WEBVTT

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.850
The Kawada yakuza group and Akagi 
are about to hold a Mahjong rematch.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.610
Today's rematch for eight 
million will take place here.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.860
Akagi's opponent is the 
blind master Ichikawa.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.870
However, Akagi has still not 
arrived at the match location.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.920
He's late.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.370
It's already past the agreed time.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.660
Why isn't he here?

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.880
We'll just have to start without him.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.380
We can't do that!

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.380
Can't we get them to postpone?

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.070
No, we can't.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.550
Everything has been set up,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.720
and Boss Miyauchi himself 
has come all the way here.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:52.890
We can't put it off.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.890
But there's no way I can beat him!

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:02.990
We may be no match for him, 
but there is a way.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.400
<b>Episode 5</b>

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.400
<b>The Treacherous Tse Ii Men</b>

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.410
Two new rules were added to this battle.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.410
1 of bamboo!

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:16.710
In consideration for Ichikawa's blindness,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:21.020
the other three players must call 
out whatever tile they are discarding.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.430
And at the end of each East-South match,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.940
if either Ichikawa or Akagi 
still have points left,

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:28.160
the game will continue into 
the West and North rounds.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.670
That's right.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.160
This is a death match that will 
continue until one of them goes bust.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.260
<b>- East Hand 1 -</b>

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.690
East Hand 1.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.260
Nangou is the North player.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.810
<i>Nangou-san, let's lay 
down our ground rules.</i>

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.650
<i>Basically, just play it straight,</i>

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.170
<i>and as soon as you have a 
two-way wait, declare riichi.</i>

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.610
<i>A tactic that mostly aims 
for self-drawn wins.</i>

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.180
<i>Don't count on other players 
to deal into your hand.</i>

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:53.410
8 of circles!

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.210
<i>As long as you don't waver, 
we still have a shot at winning.</i>

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.430
<i>It's still a closed wait.</i>

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.490
<i>I've got to make it a two-way wait.</i>

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.160
8 of characters!

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.390
<i>I've got a two-way wait!</i>

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:08.910
3 of circles!

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:09.630
Riichi!

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.600
Pinfu tsumo, 7 of circles!

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.070
At the very beginning, 
Nangou was blessed with luck.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.690
Tsumo!

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.270
Tanpin, one dora!

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.580
He wins twice in a row.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.360
At this point, Nangou has 38,200 points.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.700
Ichikawa has 20,400.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.020
An amateur like Nangou

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.810
overtaking a pro like Ichikawa 
is an unexpected development.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:39.150
But it only lasted until East Hand 2.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.330
Ichikawa had already seen 
through this strategy.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:43.830
<b>- Next Hand -</b>

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.070
<i>I have to get a two-way wait.</i>

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.310
1 of characters!

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:51.940
Ron.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.170
<i>He had a single 1 of characters wait?</i>

00:03:58.330 --> 00:03:58.840
<i>That's crazy.</i>

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.710
<i>With his discards,</i>

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.410
<i>he'd have had an ideal tanpin sanshoku
if he'd kept the 4 of characters.</i>

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.120
<i>But he deliberately threw that away 
for a single 1 of characters wait?</i>

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.620
<i>He can foresee which tiles I'll discard</i>

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.630
<i>to get out of my closed wait, 
and he's gunning for those.</i>

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.260
<i>He's going after the tiles that'll 
be left over after midgame...</i>

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.530
<i>He's onto our strategy.</i>

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.830
<i>And it only took him two hands!</i>

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.330
And then, it was East Hand 4.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.330
<b>- East Hand 4 -</b>

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.340
<i>What should I do?</i>

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:30.830
<i>Nangou!</i>

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.340
<i>If you waver now, 
you're playing into his hand!</i>

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.580
<i>This man is like every other 
man I've killed up until now.</i>

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.980
<i>He is the same species.</i>

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.830
<i>When amateurs play against us,</i>

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.300
<i>they usually follow one of two strategies.</i>

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.980
<i>They declare riichi the 
moment they're in tenpai,</i>

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.990
<i>or they go for a two-way wait, 
relying on self-drawn wins.</i>

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.330
<i>He chose the latter.</i>

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.310
<i>So all I have to do is pick off 
the tiles he gets rid of</i>

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.500
<i>from his closed waits.</i>

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.530
The tenth turn.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.240
If Nangou discards the 9 of 
characters, he'll be in tenpai.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.240
<i>Ichikawa is aiming for 
numbered tiles on the edges.</i>

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.760
<i>I can't discard this 9 of characters.</i>

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:12.980
<i>So what?</i>

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.400
<i>I have no choice.</i>

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:15.980
West wind!

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.390
Ron.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.070
<i>Chiitoitsu, with a single west wind wait!</i>

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:24.890
<i>It can't be!</i>

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.860
It's a game neither Nangou nor 
Yasuoka can afford to lose.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.210
But now they are in the 
worst possible position.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.910
They only have 2,000 points left.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:43.640
If they lose them, it's all over.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.650
<i>I'm up against the wall.</i>

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.880
Green dragon!

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:49.660
Pon!

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:52.120
7 of circles!

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.350
Chi!

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.320
<i>Now or never!</i>

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.410
<i>On the offensive again?</i>

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.560
<i>A noble effort, but too little too late.</i>

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:05.840
<i>If you wanted to win,</i>

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.570
<i>you should've played like that 
from the very first hand.</i>

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.750
Nangou is also aware that 
his efforts are futile.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.350
However, regardless of this knowledge,

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:18.810
he has no choice but to press on.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:20.850
1 of circles.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.080
Pon!

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.970
The eleventh turn.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.310
Nangou has called three 
times and is now in tenpai.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:27.440
He's waiting on the red dragon.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.920
His tenpai is like a beam of light 
momentarily penetrating the clouds.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:31.960
4 of circles!

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:33.190
Chi!

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:36.700
<i>He's in tenpai, huh?</i>

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.930
Riichi.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:43.670
Riichi!

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.940
All this happened in one turn.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.300
The momentary beam of light disappeared,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.500
suffocated by the dark clouds.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:56.960
<i>They're both in riichi...</i>

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.410
<i>And the guy after me is 
probably going for a honitsu.</i>

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:03.590
<i>I've only got these four tiles... 
Not enough to dodge their bullets.</i>

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.160
<i>All I have left is to win.</i>

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.080
<i>If I draw my winning tile now, I'll be fine!</i>

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:13.240
Nangou prays with all his might.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.510
He reaches towards the wall

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.170
as if to ask the goddess of 
victory to shake his hand.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.300
However...

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.760
The hand he ends up grabbing 
is that of the Grim Reaper.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:25.680
The dora, the white dragon.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.300
Now every tile in his hand is risky.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:35.290
The white and red dragons are risky

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:36.790
because Ichikawa is going for honor tiles,

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.190
and the player after him is going for a honitsu.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.580
The 3, 4, and 5 of circles are dangerous 
because of the player after him

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.430
and the one before him, who's declared riichi.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.390
<i>What should I discard?</i>

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.600
<i>Discarding any of these is akin to suicide.</i>

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:59.680
<i>It's basically the same 
as jumping off of a cliff.</i>

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.680
<i>It's no use... I can't choose.</i>

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:12.670
<i>Could that be....</i>

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:18.360
Akagi!

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.790
That's him, Boss.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:29.360
What took you so long, Akagi?

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.290
Nangou-san, want to switch?

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:32.160
Yes!

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.380
2,000 points left.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.410
Two people are in riichi 
and these are your tiles.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.230
Talk about being in a 
tight spot, Nangou-san.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:48.120
Yeah...

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.860
Well, in this situation, this is all we can do.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.290
Akagi, that's really...

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.070
It's fine, just be quiet.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:01.100
I'm going to make this tile pass.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:03.490
Definitely.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.310
Just then, Ichikawa was 
in a double pon wait

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.050
for a white or a red dragon.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.050
What did you discard, Akagi?

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:22.560
It's a west wind.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:26.730
<i>That's impossible!</i>

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.060
<i>A west wind?</i>

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:31.290
<i>What happened to that red dragon?</i>

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.800
<i>I could've sworn he discarded the red dragon.</i>

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.420
<i>This brat bumped it!</i>

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.700
<i>He sensed that both the white and 
red dragons were winning tiles,</i>

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:45.590
<i>so at the last moment, 
with everybody's eyes on him,</i>

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:49.120
<i>he bumped the west wind down 
while discarding the red dragon!</i>

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:54.000
This is a sleight-of-hand that makes it 
appear as if the west wind is discarded

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.400
by pushing it down with the 
red dragon tile as he discards it.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:02.120
What is so useful about this 
cheat is that it also allows one

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.480
to get rid of the dreaded 
red dragon at the same time.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:08.000
The tricks kills two birds 
with one stone instantly.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:10.770
<i>Unbelievable.</i>

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.740
<i>This is the first thing he does 
in such a major match.</i>

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.810
<i>He's pure evil!</i>

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:20.040
Looks like it passed.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.730
I will do whatever it takes.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.550
Let's take a little trip 
down to Hell, Nangou-san.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:31.860
The goddess of luck lies 
in wait beyond there.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.860
Here it comes.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:39.860
It's time for our counterattack.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:41.870
Tsumo.

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:44.770
It came!

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:45.860
Akagi!

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:52.510
Akagi's back from the 
dead with a dora tsumo.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.490
In one shot, he's managed to get 
his point box back to 16,000.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.880
And now, he has a dealer streak.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.620
We're playing until either Ichikawa 
or I completely run out of points?

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:02.240
Yeah.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.470
So you're the last dealer of South Hand 4,

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.890
but that doesn't mean it's the last hand.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.400
<i>Which means I can't win unless 
I get a direct hit on Ichikawa.</i>

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.670
<i>Still, for now I should focus 
on getting some points.</i>

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:20.900
<i>Easy points to start.</i>

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.500
As expected, the players before and 
after him do not take any risks.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.640
They're only taking part in this 
high-stake match to fill the table,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.410
and their mindset is easy to discern.

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:34.500
Their only concern is to stay inconspicuous.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.380
They do all they can to avoid 
dealing into anyone's hand

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:37.920
and stay out of the conflict.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.920
Akagi takes advantage of this 
mindset with extreme ease.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:44.430
Riichi.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.830
Against these people, 
hinting at a tanpin,

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.070
while actually having a 
hell wait, is quite effective.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:55.800
From there, they'll step 
into the trap themselves.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:56.770
Green dragon!

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:58.270
Ron.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.000
Riichi, ippatsu, iipeikou.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.000
Ippatsu - Winning in the turn after declaring riichi. 
Iipeikou - Double sequence in the same suit, e.g. 223344.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.370
There's an ura dora, too, 
so that's a dealer mangan.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.750
<i>Now I've got 28,300.</i>

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:08.980
<i>I'm safe for now.</i>

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:10.800
Then, in the next hand...

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:11.540
Chi.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:14.220
Pon.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:15.510
Kan.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:17.460
<i>Four dora!</i>

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:23.960
<i>Damn, he's lucky.</i>

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.100
8 of bamboo.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:26.930
Kan.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.700
Kan.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:31.940
1 of bamboo.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.800
<i>What kind of game is he playing?!</i>

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.710
<i>No one could've expected him 
to get so many yaku!</i>

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.570
<i>What is this?</i>

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.720
<i>He's been calling tiles like crazy, 
and now, he's up to a haneman.</i>

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:44.720
6 of bamboo.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.220
<i>Safe tile.</i>

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.230
<i>As expected, he's given up on winning.</i>

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.690
<i>Can't wait to see what he does 
when the safe tiles run out.</i>

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.930
<i>What's this kid waiting for?</i>

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.390
<i>The 1 of bamboo is a live tile.</i>

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.390
Live tile - Tile that has not yet been discarded this hand.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.700
<i>It's the perfect tile to keep for a tsumo win.</i>

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:06.660
<i>But if he discarded it, does that 
mean he wants a ron win?</i>

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:11.400
<i>But then, that means I can't discard 
any of these character or honor tiles.</i>

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.450
<i>These bamboo or circle tiles 
might be a better choice.</i>

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.450
The more he agonizes, the more 
he plays into Akagi's plan.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.340
Assuming from the discarded 1 of 
bamboo that Akagi wants a ron win,

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:25.220
he can't discard the character or honor 
tiles that Akagi knows he's holding.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.460
Which leaves the bamboo or circle tiles.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:30.730
But in this situation, their strategy

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.280
is to discard from a pair or a triplet.

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:45.500
<b>Pair                            Triplet</b>

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:36.670
Then, if they survive one discard, 
they'll have two or three safe turns.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.520
So, what tiles have they accumulated?

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:40.540
With the 4 of circles tiles used up,

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.830
tiles like the 3 of circles can no 
longer be used for sequences.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:46.830
The same goes for the tiles around 
the 8 of bamboo, namely the 7 and 9.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:51.630
In any case, there is no surefire 
way to avoid a single wait.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:54.350
<i>Come on.</i>

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:55.920
<i>I'll take it.</i>

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.070
Is Akagi's insight actually 
pulling the tile towards him?

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:03.430
Or is this the work of something 
entirely different, something demonic?

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.430
He will discard it.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.160
As if possessed...

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:10.670
3 of circles.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:14.190
Ron.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:15.860
San kantsu, four dora.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:15.860
San kantsu - A hand of three kans.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:16.610
Haneman.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.560
<i>He did it!</i>

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.710
<i>He's at 46,900.</i>

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:23.380
You did it, Akagi!

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.540
You're truly a genius.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:27.070
We can always count on you.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.720
There's still one big nuisance 
sitting across from me.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:34.390
We can celebrate once I've 
properly crushed him.

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:36.000
You're a ballsy kid.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:40.040
You took out the small fry, 
and you built up a stash of points.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:43.230
I guess it's time for the main event, eh?

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:45.970
I guess so.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.970
I'm going to take you out.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:53.980
It's a match between Ichikawa and Akagi.

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:58.110
A death match that will continue 
until one of them loses all of his points.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.480
He needs to get a direct 
hit off of Ichikawa...

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:03.760
A near-impossible task.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.540
This is where the match really begins.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:09.690
And this is Akagi's first discard.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:11.690
3 of circles.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:12.430
Huh?

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.210
<i>He's discarding the 3 of circles
from a hand like that?</i>

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.400
<i>Why?</i>

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:20.630
And again, on the second turn.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.240
And on the third turn.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:24.370
Three 3 of circles discards in a row.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.380
No one can understand 
what he's trying to do.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:32.380
But Ichikawa alone understands.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.580
<i>Hey, hey, what are you doing?</i>

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:41.420
<i>I get it!</i>

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:42.820
<i>He determined that it'll be</i>

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.500
<i>too difficult to get direct hits 
off each other in a normal game,</i>

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:49.330
<i>so he is purging an entire 
suit from his hand on purpose.</i>

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:54.350
<i>Akagi threw down the gauntlet, 
and Ichikawa accepted his challenge.</i>

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:57.600
<b>Tse Ii Men</b>

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:57.020
Purging an entire suit, Tse Ii Men...

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:58.820
Known in Japanese as "zetsu ichimon."

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:02.210
Once both players eliminate 
one suit from their hands,

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:07.080
the tiles left when one is in tenpai will 
naturally be from the other two suits.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:10.240
Usually the players end up 
trading blows until one falls.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:11.820
Essentially, it's Mahjong 
where you can't run away.

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:17.460
Comparatively, regular 
Mahjong is like out-boxing.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.730
While you fight, you keep 
an escape route ready.

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:26.100
But a zetsu ichimon game is 
like in-fighting at close range,

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.970
with both your feet planted.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.080
<i>This is insane.</i>

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:33.130
<i>Ignoring the circles right from the start...</i>

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.940
<i>They're going to be waiting on 
bamboo or character tiles.</i>

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:38.680
But on the 13th turn...

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:40.910
2 of circles.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.910
Akagi discards a 2 of circles from his hand.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.870
<i>Why is he discarding a 
2 of circles from his hand now?</i>

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:48.920
<i>Wait... I see.</i>

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:53.540
<i>He was trying to play us from the start!</i>

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.930
<i>In a zetsu ichimon game, 
one suit gets purged,</i>

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.580
<i>so that suit inevitably becomes safe.</i>

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:03.010
<i>But if he uses that principle against us</i>

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:06.220
<i>and waits long enough, 
there's no better camouflage.</i>

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:09.170
<i>He was saving his 2 of circles for that.</i>

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.710
<i>But the next player has 
already discarded one,</i>

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.450
<i>and the previous player two, 
eliminating all of them.</i>

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.970
<i>He had no choice but to give up 
on his single 2 of circles wait.</i>

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:22.500
<i>Now he's probably in a multi-sided 
wait for a bamboo or character tile.</i>

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.960
<i>Man, you can't let your 
guard down with this kid.</i>

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:32.430
Ron.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:34.880
Ittsuu, one dora.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:34.880
Ittsuu - Full straight, 1-9 of one suit.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:36.430
Three consecutive wins, so 8,600.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.090
<i>A single 1 of circles wait?!</i>

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:40.940
<i>How could he wait on that?!</i>

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.030
<i>He just won't go down.</i>

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:46.750
<i>He pretended to give up his single 
wait by discarding the 2 of circles,</i>

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:50.950
<i>but was still concealing another 
single wait for a circle tile!</i>

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:55.080
<i>This is demonic... This isn't 
something a kid could come up with!</i>

00:17:55.780 --> 00:18:01.060
In their first zetsu ichimon match, 
Akagi outfoxes Ichikawa.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.320
It's an auspicious start.

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:05.650
This was the start of their

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.270
zetsu ichimon battle that 
had no end in sight...

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:10.330
Akagi continues to masterfully

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:14.700
utilize waits that circumvent 
the principles of zetsu ichimon.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:17.480
At first, it seems that 
Ichikawa is being played

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:20.200
by Akagi's varied and 
unpredictable playing style,

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.580
but that is not the case.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:24.500
Tsumo.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.810
Ichikawa is orthodox.

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:29.810
He does not insist on a direct hit on Akagi.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:32.770
He frequently calls tsumo.

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:34.450
And then...

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.460
The fate of zetsu ichimon...

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.670
The inevitable outflow of certain tiles...

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.030
2 of circles.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:43.510
Ron.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:44.530
He's out to kill.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.340
Tanpin, two dora, mangan.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:51.010
They were both playing 
zetsu ichimon matches,

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:54.000
but their playing styles couldn't 
have been more different.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:59.950
Akagi favors change, while Ichikawa 
prefers to stay true to the basics.

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:04.620
But eventually, the gap widens...

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:08.120
9 of characters.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:09.860
Ron.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.600
Pinfu, iipeikou, two dora.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:17.860
Mangan.

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:19.820
No problem.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.050
It's no biggie.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.970
That much is expected in 
a game of zetsu ichimon.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:27.370
I don't think so, Nangou-san.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:30.180
Akagi is suffering.

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:34.300
I thought that too, in the beginning,

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:38.980
but now, Ichikawa is the 
one with the points.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.030
They've both won the 
same amount of times,

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:45.320
but the value of each win is different.

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:48.880
It's only natural, considering that 
Akagi mostly uses irregular waits

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.590
that make his hands worth less.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.520
Meanwhile, Ichikawa is 
constantly building his hands

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.230
on wins that mean much more in Mahjong.

00:19:57.230 --> 00:20:02.240
True, his style isn't 
exciting, but he wins big.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:06.370
In a straight race for points, 
there's no way Akagi can win.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:08.890
He's letting Akagi stand in the spotlight,

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:10.980
while reaping the profits for himself.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:12.220
3 of characters.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:13.720
Ron.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:15.360
Tanpin, sanshoku.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:17.640
Mangan.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:20.590
<i>That's right.</i>

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:23.460
<i>Have you realized it now, Akagi-kun?</i>

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:27.730
<i>It's nonsensical to scheme 
elaborate waits in zetsu ichimon.</i>

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:31.470
<i>What needs to flow out 
will flow out regardless.</i>

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:35.430
<i>I'll leave the skillful Mahjong to you.</i>

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:37.010
<i>I don't mind being unskilled...</i>

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:38.980
<i>I'll just win.</i>

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:43.480
<i>Since you prefer that kind of 
showy style, you can't beat me.</i>

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:48.620
<i>To defeat me, you'd have to 
overturn the most basic rules</i>

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.530
<i>of Mahjong itself, and not 
even God can do that.</i>

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:56.270
Ichikawa is unaffected by 
Akagi's aggressive attacks.

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:59.060
He is truly like an impregnable fortress.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:03.500
Akagi seems to be caught 
in his own trap...

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:07.530
Does he have a plan to get to victory?

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.700
Next Time: "The Picaro's Talent."

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:20.510
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:20.510
<b>The Picaro's Talent</b>
